WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.408
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.770
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:17.213
Unit fifty-nine: Peace and Nonviolence. In this unit, you will critically evaluate nonviolent approaches to conflict.

4
00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:19.473
nonviolence.

5
00:00:20.173 --> 00:00:25.992
nonviolence means the principle of refusing to use physical force, even against injustice.

6
00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:30.103
Nonviolence guided Gandhi's struggle for Indian independence.

7
00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:33.920
Your turn. Say: nonviolence.

8
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.830
nonviolence.

9
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:39.795
pacifism.

10
00:00:40.495 --> 00:00:44.804
pacifism means the belief that war and violence are never justified.

11
00:00:45.104 --> 00:00:49.798
Pacifism is admired by some and criticised as unrealistic by others.

12
00:00:50.498 --> 00:00:53.568
Your turn. Say: pacifism.

13
00:00:56.268 --> 00:00:57.292
pacifism.

14
00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:00.120
conflict transformation.

15
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:08.044
conflict transformation means changing the relationships and causes behind a conflict, not just stopping the fighting.

16
00:01:08.344 --> 00:01:11.969
Conflict transformation brought former enemies to work together.

17
00:01:12.669 --> 00:01:16.146
Your turn. Say: conflict transformation.

18
00:01:18.846 --> 00:01:20.637
conflict transformation.

19
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.557
reconciliation process.

20
00:01:24.257 --> 00:01:29.902
reconciliation process means the slow work of restoring trust between groups after violence.

21
00:01:30.202 --> 00:01:34.686
Truth-telling is often the first step in a reconciliation process.

22
00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:39.408
Your turn. Say: reconciliation process.

23
00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:44.119
reconciliation process.

24
00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:46.594
peace education.

25
00:01:47.294 --> 00:01:51.906
peace education means teaching skills and values that prevent violence.

26
00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:56.376
Peace education teaches students to solve disagreements calmly.

27
00:01:57.076 --> 00:02:00.483
Your turn. Say: peace education.

28
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:04.521
peace education.

29
00:02:05.517 --> 00:02:07.261
nonviolent resistance.

30
00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:15.394
nonviolent resistance means opposing injustice through protests, strikes and civil disobedience without violence.

31
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:19.191
Nonviolent resistance has toppled several dictatorships.

32
00:02:19.891 --> 00:02:23.809
Your turn. Say: nonviolent resistance.

33
00:02:26.509 --> 00:02:28.299
nonviolent resistance.

34
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:30.505
culture of peace.

35
00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:37.535
culture of peace means values and ways of life that reject violence and promote dialogue.

36
00:02:37.835 --> 00:02:41.042
UNESCO promotes a culture of peace in schools.

37
00:02:41.742 --> 00:02:44.766
Your turn. Say: culture of peace.

38
00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:48.908
culture of peace.

39
00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:51.173
positive peace.

40
00:02:51.873 --> 00:02:57.507
positive peace means not only the absence of war but the presence of justice and well-being.

41
00:02:57.807 --> 00:03:01.223
Positive peace requires fair schools, courts and jobs.

42
00:03:01.923 --> 00:03:05.364
Your turn. Say: positive peace.

43
00:03:08.064 --> 00:03:09.402
positive peace.

44
00:03:10.417 --> 00:03:12.579
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:17.293
Ms. Sarah, does nonviolence really work against powerful regimes?

46
00:03:18.583 --> 00:03:27.273
The evidence is surprisingly strong. One major study found nonviolent campaigns succeeded about twice as often as violent ones.

47
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:33.151
That's remarkable. But isn't pacifism naïve when facing aggression?

48
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:41.781
That is a fair criticism. Absolute pacifism is, arguably, hard to defend in every case.

49
00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:47.486
Cambodia has its own example: Maha Ghosananda's peace walks.

50
00:03:48.783 --> 00:03:54.118
A compelling example. He showed that reconciliation can begin with simple steps.

51
00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:57.625
So stopping war isn't enough?

52
00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:06.680
Precisely. Negative peace ends the fighting; positive peace builds justice. The latter is far more demanding.

53
00:04:07.983 --> 00:04:12.084
Grammar focus: Critical evaluation language (advanced).

54
00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:16.114
Critical evaluation weighs strengths and weaknesses.

55
00:04:16.414 --> 00:04:23.362
The evidence for nonviolence is surprisingly strong. That is a fair criticism of absolute pacifism.

56
00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:27.275
Maha Ghosananda's peace walks are a compelling example.

57
00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:34.706
Evaluate sources and arguments explicitly: say whether evidence is strong, limited or mixed, and why.

58
00:04:35.717 --> 00:04:38.996
Now read and listen. The Strength of Nonviolence.

59
00:04:39.296 --> 00:04:44.140
Many people assume that violence is the most effective way to resist oppression.

60
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:58.328
Research by Erica Chenoweth and Maria Stephan, however, found that nonviolent resistance campaigns succeeded about twice as often as violent ones between 1900 and 2006.

61
00:04:59.617 --> 00:05:06.992
Nonviolence attracts broader participation and makes repression more costly for regimes.

62
00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:16.030
Critics argue that strict pacifism cannot stop every aggressor, a criticism that deserves serious consideration.

63
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:25.342
Scholar Johan Galtung distinguished negative peace, the absence of war, from positive peace, the presence of justice.

64
00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:32.910
Achieving positive peace requires conflict transformation and a long reconciliation process.

65
00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:43.530
In Cambodia, the monk Maha Ghosananda led peace walks from 1992, offering a compelling model of healing after war.

66
00:05:44.817 --> 00:05:51.948
Peace education and a culture of peace may therefore be the most powerful long-term investments a society can make.

67
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.937
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:56.237 --> 00:05:59.096
Peace that includes justice and well-being is blank.

69
00:05:59.396 --> 00:06:05.244
A, positive peace. B, pacifism. C, nonviolence.

70
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.225
The answer is A: positive peace.

71
00:06:13.717 --> 00:06:16.668
Which phrase concedes a weakness in your own argument?

72
00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:22.909
A, That is a fair criticism. B, The evidence is overwhelming. C, Clearly.

73
00:06:26.917 --> 00:06:30.333
The answer is A: That is a fair criticism.

74
00:06:31.817 --> 00:06:34.629
'A compelling example' is an example that is blank.

75
00:06:34.929 --> 00:06:39.325
A, very convincing. B, boring. C, false.

76
00:06:43.317 --> 00:06:46.361
The answer is A: very convincing.

77
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.359
Restoring trust between groups after violence is a blank.

78
00:06:51.659 --> 00:06:58.052
A, reconciliation process. B, culture of peace. C, peace education.

79
00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.443
The answer is A: reconciliation process.

80
00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:12.967
According to the text, how often did nonviolent campaigns succeed compared with violent ones?

81
00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:18.302
A, about twice as often. B, about half as often. C, equally often.

82
00:07:22.317 --> 00:07:25.210
The answer is A: about twice as often.

83
00:07:26.717 --> 00:07:32.573
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is nonviolence always the right response to injustice?

84
00:07:37.873 --> 00:07:44.076
Part two: Evaluate the evidence, concede one criticism and give a compelling example.

85
00:07:50.376 --> 00:08:04.211
The evidence for nonviolent resistance is surprisingly strong. That said, critics make a fair point about defending people from immediate attack. Maha Ghosananda's peace walks are a compelling example of nonviolence healing a nation.

86
00:08:04.511 --> 00:08:06.731
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:08:07.717 --> 00:08:13.295
Great work today! You learned eight new words and critical evaluation language (advanced).

88
00:08:13.595 --> 00:08:17.164
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:08:17.464 --> 00:08:20.047
See you in Unit sixty. Goodbye!

90
00:08:20.347 --> 00:08:23.498
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

